From: Petr Wozniak <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] map-controller: one column, one unit
Signed-off-by: Petr Wozniak <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>

sta_metrics_hist.dl_rate carried two units at once. Measured 2026-09-06 on a
live mesh: 940, 109 and 88 sitting next to 65000 and 450567 - a thousandfold
apart, and every one of them readable as a rate.

The two writers take their value from two different TLVs:

  Extended Link Metrics TLV   struct sta_ext_link_metric_bss.dl_rate
                              Last Data Downlink Rate, Kbps
                              -> the client-level row (cntlr_map.c:2712, :3331)

  Link Metrics TLV            struct assoc_sta_link_metrics_bss.dl_thput
                              Estimated MAC Data Rate, Mb/s
                              -> the per-leg row (this call site)

Both land in wifi_stamld_element.dl_rate, whose declaration says "latest data
rate in Kbps" (wifi_dataelements.h:438). One of them is telling the truth.

Within one series nothing was visibly wrong, which is why this survived:
sta_trend groups by (macaddr, bssid), so a leg curve is all estimates and a
client curve is all measurements, each internally consistent. What was wrong
is everything that compares two series, or prints the number with a unit
after it - and a graph drawn from this column would have looked entirely
plausible while doing exactly that.

Converted where the value enters, so the field means what its declaration
says. Nothing downstream changes; the readers already treat it as Kbps.

NOT fixed here, and worth saying: the two writers still put two different
QUANTITIES in one column - an estimate of what the link could do, and a
measurement of what it did. Per series that is harmless, since each series
has one writer. Before this column is ever plotted across series it needs
either a source marker or a second column.

Verify: on a live mesh, per-leg rows in sta_metrics_hist must be of the same
magnitude as the client-level rows for a link of comparable speed - hundreds
of thousands, not hundreds.
--- a/src/cntlr_map.c
+++ b/src/cntlr_map.c
@@ -3245,11 +3245,31 @@
 			}
 
 #if (EASYMESH_VERSION >= 6)
-			/* Independent of sta_table: this is keyed by the leg. */
+			/* Independent of sta_table: this is keyed by the leg.
+			 *
+			 * dl_thput/ul_thput are the Estimated MAC Data Rate of
+			 * the Associated STA Link Metrics TLV, and that field is
+			 * Mb/s. It lands in wifi_stamld_element.dl_rate, which
+			 * says "latest data rate in Kbps" and is filled that way
+			 * everywhere else - from the Extended Link Metrics TLV,
+			 * which really is Kbps (cntlr_map.c:2712, :3331).
+			 *
+			 * So one column carried two units. Measured 2026-09-06
+			 * in sta_metrics_hist: 940 and 109 next to 65000 and
+			 * 450567, a thousandfold apart, and both readable as a
+			 * rate. Per series it is still homogeneous - sta_trend
+			 * groups by (macaddr, bssid) - so nothing was wrong
+			 * within one curve; anything comparing two curves, or
+			 * printing a number with a unit, was.
+			 *
+			 * Convert where the value enters, so the field means what
+			 * its declaration says. A graph drawn from this column
+			 * would have looked entirely plausible.
+			 */
 			cntlr_affiliated_sta_metrics(c, p->macaddr, b->bssid,
 						     b->ul_rcpi,
-						     BUF_GET_BE32(b->dl_thput),
-						     BUF_GET_BE32(b->ul_thput));
+						     BUF_GET_BE32(b->dl_thput) * 1000,
+						     BUF_GET_BE32(b->ul_thput) * 1000);
 #endif
 
 			if (!s) {
